import Mathlib

/-
# Atiyah–Macdonald, *Introduction to Commutative Algebra* (1969), Exercise 4.15

## The statement in the book

> **15.** Let `a` be a decomposable ideal in a ring `A`, let `Σ` be an isolated set of prime
> ideals belonging to `a`, and let `q_Σ` be the intersection of the corresponding primary
> components. Let `f` be an element of `A` such that, for each prime ideal `𝔭` belonging to
> `a`, we have `f ∈ 𝔭 ⟺ 𝔭 ∉ Σ`, and let `S_f` be the set of all powers of `f`. Show that
> `q_Σ = S_f(a) = (a : fⁿ)` for all large `n`.

(Here `S_f(a)` is the saturation of `a` with respect to `S_f`, i.e. the contraction in `A` of
the extension `S_f⁻¹a`, and `(a : fⁿ)` is the ideal quotient `{x | x fⁿ ∈ a}`; see
Atiyah–Macdonald, §4, Exercise 12 for the notation `S(a)`, and (3.11) for contractions of
extensions.)

## Formalisation

We consider a ring `A`, a family `q : ι → Ideal A` indexed by a finite type `ι` with all `q i`
primary and with pairwise distinct radicals `𝔭ᵢ = √(qᵢ)` (hypothesis `_hmin`), together with a
finite set `S` of indices (playing the role of `Σ`, the isolated set of prime ideals belonging
to `a`), an element `f : A` and the ideal `a = ⋂ i, q i` (hypothesis `hdecomp`), i.e. a
(minimal) primary decomposition of `a`. The hypothesis `hf : ∀ i, f ∈ √(q i) ↔ i ∉ S` is the
book's condition that `f ∈ 𝔭 ⟺ 𝔭 ∉ Σ` for all prime ideals `𝔭` belonging to `a`. The
conclusion is

  `S_f(a) = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`  and  `(a : fⁿ) = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`  for all sufficiently large `n`.

Here `saturation a f` is literally the contraction `(a.map (algebraMap A (Localization.Away f))).under A`
of the extension of `a` to the localization at the submonoid generated by `f`, and `(a : fⁿ)` is
`a.colon {fⁿ}` (`Submodule.colon` by the singleton `{fⁿ}`, which coincides with the colon by
the principal ideal `(fⁿ)` by `Submodule.colon_span`).

The hypotheses `_hmin`, `_hirr` (the two clauses of minimality of the decomposition) and
`_hisolated` (isolatedness of `Σ`) are part of the book's hypotheses. The argument below does
not need them: it works for *any* primary decomposition together with the set `Σ` determined by
`f`. Note that the exercise's hypothesis on `f` does force `Σ` to be isolated — see
`isolated_of_forall` below.

## Proof (the book's argument, made explicit)

Localising at `S_f` kills exactly the components `qᵢ` with `f ∈ 𝔭ᵢ`, that is, those with
`i ∉ Σ`; hence contracting back gives `⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`. Concretely, `x ∈ S_f(a)` iff `f^n x ∈ a`
for some `n`, and one checks that `∃ n, ∀ i, fⁿ x ∈ qᵢ` iff `∀ i ∈ Σ, x ∈ qᵢ`: for `i ∈ Σ` one
has `f ∉ 𝔭ᵢ`, so `f` is a non-zero-divisor modulo `qᵢ`.

For the second identity, `(a : fⁿ) = ⋂ i, (qᵢ : fⁿ)`. If `f ∉ 𝔭ᵢ` (`i ∈ Σ`) then
`(qᵢ : fⁿ) = qᵢ` for every `n`; if `f ∈ 𝔭ᵢ` (`i ∉ Σ`) then `f^{mᵢ} ∈ qᵢ` for some `mᵢ`, so
`(qᵢ : fⁿ) = ⊤` for `n ≥ mᵢ`. Taking `n` at least the maximum of the finitely many `mᵢ` gives
`(a : fⁿ) = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`. (Note: the second half of the published natural solution only
records `(a : fⁿ) ⊆ S_f(a)`, which holds for all `n`; the equality *for large `n`* claimed in
the book needs the argument just given. Both halves are proved below.)

## Non-vacuity

`solution_int` below instantiates the main theorem with the classical example
`A = ℤ`, `a = (12) = (4) ∩ (3)`, `Σ = {(3)}`, `f = 2`, and derives `S_2((12)) = (3)` and
`(12 : 2²) = (3)`; `colon_one_int` shows `(12 : 2) = (6)`, so "for all large `n`" cannot be
improved to "for all `n`" here.
-/

open scoped Pointwise

namespace AtiyahMcdonald69

namespace Ex_4_15

variable {A : Type*} [CommRing A]

/-- `S_f(a)`: the saturation of `a` with respect to the multiplicative subset
`S_f = {1, f, f², …}` generated by `f`, that is, the contraction in `A` of the extension of
`a` to the localization `S_f⁻¹A = A_f`. -/
noncomputable def saturation (a : Ideal A) (f : A) : Ideal A :=
  (a.map (algebraMap A (Localization.Away f))).under A

/-- Membership in the saturation: `x ∈ S_f(a)` iff `fⁿ x ∈ a` for some `n`. -/
theorem mem_saturation {a : Ideal A} {f x : A} :
    x ∈ saturation a f ↔ ∃ n : ℕ, f ^ n * x ∈ a := by
  rw [saturation, Ideal.mem_under,
    IsLocalization.algebraMap_mem_map_algebraMap_iff (Submonoid.powers f) (Localization.Away f)
      a x]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨m, hm, hx⟩
    obtain ⟨n, rfl⟩ := (Submonoid.mem_powers_iff m f).mp hm
    exact ⟨n, hx⟩
  · rintro ⟨n, hx⟩
    exact ⟨f ^ n, (Submonoid.mem_powers_iff (f ^ n) f).mpr ⟨n, rfl⟩, hx⟩

/-- If `f` is not in a prime ideal `𝔭`, no power of `f` lies in `𝔭`. -/
theorem pow_notMem_of_notMem {p : Ideal A} (hp : p.IsPrime) {f : A} (hf : f ∉ p) (n : ℕ) :
    f ^ n ∉ p :=
  fun h => hf (hp.mem_of_pow_mem n h)

/-- For a primary ideal `q`, an element `f ∉ √q` is a non-zero-divisor modulo `q`. -/
theorem mem_of_mul_mem_of_notMem_radical {q : Ideal A} (hq : q.IsPrimary) {f x : A}
    (hf : f ∉ q.radical) (h : x * f ∈ q) : x ∈ q := by
  rcases (Ideal.isPrimary_iff.mp hq).2 h with h' | h'
  · exact h'
  · exact absurd h' hf

/-- The set of prime ideals belonging to `a` that do not contain `f` is isolated: the
hypothesis on `Σ` in the exercise is automatic once `f` has the stated property. -/
theorem isolated_of_forall {ι : Type*} (q : ι → Ideal A) (S : Finset ι) (f : A)
    (hf : ∀ i, f ∈ (q i).radical ↔ i ∉ S) :
    ∀ i ∈ S, ∀ j, (q j).radical ≤ (q i).radical → j ∈ S := by
  intro i hi j hle
  have hfnot : f ∉ (q i).radical := fun h => (hf i).mp h hi
  by_contra hj
  exact hfnot (hle ((hf j).mpr hj))

/-- **Atiyah–Macdonald, Exercise 4.15.** Let `a = ⋂ᵢ qᵢ` be a minimal primary decomposition of
`a` (with `qᵢ` primary and the radicals `√qᵢ` pairwise distinct), let `Σ` be an isolated set of
indices (prime ideals belonging to `a`) and `q_Σ = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`, and let `f ∈ A` satisfy
`f ∈ √qᵢ ↔ i ∉ Σ` for all `i`. Then `q_Σ = S_f(a) = (a : fⁿ)` for all sufficiently large `n`. -/
theorem solution {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι]
    (a : Ideal A) (q : ι → Ideal A) (S : Finset ι) (f : A)
    (hprimary : ∀ i, (q i).IsPrimary)
    (_hmin : ∀ i j, (q i).radical = (q j).radical → i = j)
    (_hirr : ∀ i, ¬ (⨅ j ∈ ({i}ᶜ : Set ι), q j) ≤ q i)
    (hdecomp : a = ⨅ i, q i)
    (_hisolated : ∀ i ∈ S, ∀ j, (q j).radical ≤ (q i).radical → j ∈ S)
    (hf : ∀ i, f ∈ (q i).radical ↔ i ∉ S) :
    saturation a f = ⨅ i ∈ S, q i ∧
      ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ n : ℕ, N ≤ n → a.colon {f ^ n} = ⨅ i ∈ S, q i := by
  classical
  -- For every `i` with `f ∈ √qᵢ` pick an exponent `e i` with `f ^ (e i) ∈ qᵢ`; `N` bounds them.
  have hexp : ∀ i : ι, ∃ n : ℕ, i ∉ S → f ^ n ∈ q i := by
    intro i
    by_cases hi : i ∉ S
    · obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := Ideal.mem_radical_iff.mp ((hf i).mpr hi)
      exact ⟨n, fun _ => hn⟩
    · exact ⟨0, fun h => absurd h hi⟩
  choose e he using hexp
  let N : ℕ := Finset.univ.sup e
  have he_le : ∀ i : ι, e i ≤ N := fun i =>
    Finset.le_sup (s := Finset.univ) (f := e) (Finset.mem_univ i)
  have hN : ∀ i : ι, i ∉ S → f ^ N ∈ q i := by
    intro i hi
    have hle : e i ≤ N := he_le i
    have hpow : f ^ N = f ^ (N - e i) * f ^ (e i) := by
      rw [← pow_add, Nat.sub_add_cancel hle]
    rw [hpow]
    exact (q i).mul_mem_left _ (he i hi)
  -- The key characterization of the components indexed by `S`, for exponents `n ≥ N`.
  have key : ∀ (n : ℕ), N ≤ n → ∀ x : A,
      ((∀ i : ι, f ^ n * x ∈ q i) ↔ ∀ i : ι, i ∈ S → x ∈ q i) := by
    intro n hn x
    constructor
    · intro h i hi
      have hfrad : f ∉ (q i).radical := fun hh => (hf i).mp hh hi
      have hmul : x * f ^ n ∈ q i := by rw [mul_comm]; exact h i
      exact mem_of_mul_mem_of_notMem_radical (hprimary i)
        (pow_notMem_of_notMem (Ideal.isPrime_radical (hprimary i)) hfrad n) hmul
    · intro hx i
      by_cases hi : i ∈ S
      · exact (q i).mul_mem_left _ (hx i hi)
      · have hle : e i ≤ n := le_trans (he_le i) hn
        have hpow : f ^ n = f ^ (n - e i) * f ^ (e i) := by
          rw [← pow_add, Nat.sub_add_cancel hle]
        rw [hpow, mul_assoc]
        exact (q i).mul_mem_left _ ((q i).mul_mem_right x (he i hi))
  -- Membership in `⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`.
  have hmem : ∀ x : A, (x ∈ ⨅ i ∈ S, q i) ↔ ∀ i : ι, i ∈ S → x ∈ q i := fun x => by simp
  constructor
  -- First identity: `S_f(a) = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ`.
  · ext x
    rw [mem_saturation, hmem x]
    have h1 : (∃ m : ℕ, f ^ m * x ∈ a) ↔ ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ i : ι, f ^ m * x ∈ q i := by
      refine exists_congr fun m => ?_
      rw [hdecomp, Ideal.mem_iInf]
    rw [h1]
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨m, hm⟩
      by_cases hm' : N ≤ m
      · exact (key m hm' x).mp hm
      · have hmle : m ≤ N := le_of_lt (not_le.mp hm')
        have hfac : f ^ N = f ^ (N - m) * f ^ m := by
          rw [← pow_add, Nat.sub_add_cancel hmle]
        have hall : ∀ i : ι, f ^ N * x ∈ q i := by
          intro i
          rw [hfac, mul_assoc]
          exact (q i).mul_mem_left _ (hm i)
        exact (key N le_rfl x).mp hall
    · rintro hx
      exact ⟨N, (key N le_rfl x).mpr hx⟩
  -- Second identity: `(a : fⁿ) = ⋂_{i ∈ Σ} qᵢ` for all `n ≥ N`.
  · refine ⟨N, fun n hn => ?_⟩
    have key2 : ∀ x : A, (x * f ^ n ∈ a ↔ ∀ i : ι, i ∈ S → x ∈ q i) := by
      intro x
      have h2 : (x * f ^ n ∈ a) ↔ ∀ i : ι, x * f ^ n ∈ q i := by
        rw [hdecomp, Ideal.mem_iInf]
      rw [h2]
      constructor
      · intro h i hi
        have hfrad : f ∉ (q i).radical := fun hh => (hf i).mp hh hi
        exact mem_of_mul_mem_of_notMem_radical (hprimary i)
          (pow_notMem_of_notMem (Ideal.isPrime_radical (hprimary i)) hfrad n) (h i)
      · intro hx i
        rw [mul_comm]
        exact (key n hn x).mpr hx i
    ext x
    rw [Submodule.mem_colon_singleton, smul_eq_mul, key2 x, hmem x]

/-! ### Non-vacuity: the decomposition `(12) = (4) ∩ (3)` in `ℤ`

The hypotheses of the exercise are satisfiable, and the identities of the exercise can be
checked by hand in the classical example `A = ℤ`, `a = (12) = (4) ∩ (3)`, `Σ = {(3)}`,
`f = 2`: here `q_Σ = (3)`, `S_2((12)) = (3)` and `(12 : 2ⁿ) = (3)` for all `n ≥ 2`
(while `(12 : 2) = (6)`, so the "for all large `n`" is sharp). The instance below
instantiates `solution` with this data and derives the two identities concretely. -/

/-- The two primary components of `(12) = (4) ∩ (3)` in `ℤ`, indexed by `Fin 2`:
`q 0 = (4)` is `(2)`-primary and `q 1 = (3)` is prime. -/
private def qZ : Fin 2 → Ideal ℤ := ![Ideal.span {(4 : ℤ)}, Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)}]

private lemma prime_span_two : (Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)}).IsPrime :=
  (Ideal.span_singleton_prime (by norm_num : (2 : ℤ) ≠ 0)).mpr (by norm_num)

private lemma prime_span_three : (Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)}).IsPrime :=
  (Ideal.span_singleton_prime (by norm_num : (3 : ℤ) ≠ 0)).mpr (by norm_num)

private lemma qZ_radical_zero : (qZ 0).radical = Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)} := by
  show (Ideal.span {(4 : ℤ)}).radical = Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)}
  have h4 : Ideal.span {(4 : ℤ)} = Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)} ^ 2 := by
    rw [Ideal.span_singleton_pow]; norm_num
  rw [h4, Ideal.radical_pow (Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)}) (by norm_num : (2 : ℕ) ≠ 0),
    prime_span_two.radical]

private lemma qZ_radical_one : (qZ 1).radical = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} := by
  show (Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)}).radical = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)}
  exact prime_span_three.radical

private lemma qZ_primary_zero : (qZ 0).IsPrimary := by
  apply Ideal.isPrimary_of_isMaximal_radical
  rw [qZ_radical_zero]
  exact prime_span_two.isMaximal (fun h => by
    have h1 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ (⊥ : Ideal ℤ) := by
      rw [← h]; exact Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self 2
    simp at h1)

private lemma qZ_primary_one : (qZ 1).IsPrimary := by
  rw [show qZ 1 = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} from rfl]
  exact prime_span_three.isPrimary

private lemma two_ne_three : (Ideal.span {(2 : ℤ)}) ≠ Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} := by
  intro h
  have h2 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} := by
    rw [← h]; exact Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self 2
  rw [Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at h2
  omega

private lemma twelve_eq : Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)} = qZ 0 ⊓ qZ 1 := by
  rw [show qZ 0 = Ideal.span {(4 : ℤ)} from rfl, show qZ 1 = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} from rfl]
  ext x
  simp only [Ideal.mem_inf, Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨a, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨⟨3 * a, by ring⟩, ⟨4 * a, by ring⟩⟩
  · rintro ⟨⟨a, ha⟩, ⟨b, hb⟩⟩
    exact ⟨x / 12, by omega⟩

/-- The intersection of the components indexed by `Σ = {1}` is `q_Σ = (3)`. -/
private lemma inf_singleton_one :
    (⨅ i ∈ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)), qZ i) = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} := by
  ext x
  simp only [Ideal.mem_iInf, Finset.mem_singleton]
  exact ⟨fun h => h 1 rfl, fun h i hi => hi ▸ h⟩

/-- In `ℤ`, with `a = (12) = (4) ∩ (3)`, `Σ = {(3)}` and `f = 2`, the conclusion of
Exercise 4.15 reads `S_2((12)) = (3)` and `(12 : 2ⁿ) = (3)` for all large `n`
(here already for `n ≥ 2`). -/
theorem solution_int :
    saturation (Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)}) 2 = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} ∧
      (Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)}).colon {2 ^ 2} = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} := by
  have hprimary : ∀ i, (qZ i).IsPrimary := by
    intro i; fin_cases i
    · exact qZ_primary_zero
    · exact qZ_primary_one
  have hmin : ∀ i j, (qZ i).radical = (qZ j).radical → i = j := by
    intro i j hij
    fin_cases i
    · fin_cases j
      · rfl
      · exact absurd (by rw [← qZ_radical_zero, ← qZ_radical_one]; exact hij) two_ne_three
    · fin_cases j
      · exact absurd (by rw [← qZ_radical_zero, ← qZ_radical_one]; exact hij.symm) two_ne_three
      · rfl
  have hdecomp : Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)} = ⨅ i, qZ i := by
    rw [twelve_eq]
    ext x
    simp only [Ideal.mem_iInf, Ideal.mem_inf]
    exact ⟨fun h i => by fin_cases i; exacts [h.1, h.2], fun h => ⟨h 0, h 1⟩⟩
  have hirr : ∀ i, ¬ (⨅ j ∈ ({i}ᶜ : Set (Fin 2)), qZ j) ≤ qZ i := by
    intro i
    fin_cases i
    · intro hle
      have h3 : (3 : ℤ) ∈ (⨅ j ∈ ({0}ᶜ : Set (Fin 2)), qZ j) := by
        simp only [Ideal.mem_iInf, Set.mem_compl_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
        intro j hj
        fin_cases j
        · exact absurd rfl hj
        · simp [qZ]
      have hmem : (3 : ℤ) ∈ qZ 0 := hle h3
      rw [show qZ 0 = Ideal.span {(4 : ℤ)} from rfl, Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at hmem
      omega
    · intro hle
      have h4 : (4 : ℤ) ∈ (⨅ j ∈ ({1}ᶜ : Set (Fin 2)), qZ j) := by
        simp only [Ideal.mem_iInf, Set.mem_compl_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
        intro j hj
        fin_cases j
        · simp [qZ]
        · exact absurd rfl hj
      have hmem : (4 : ℤ) ∈ qZ 1 := hle h4
      rw [show qZ 1 = Ideal.span {(3 : ℤ)} from rfl, Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at hmem
      omega
  have hisolated :
      ∀ i ∈ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)), ∀ j, (qZ j).radical ≤ (qZ i).radical →
        j ∈ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)) := by
    intro i hi j hle
    simp only [Finset.mem_singleton] at hi ⊢
    subst hi
    fin_cases j
    · exfalso
      have h2 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ (qZ 0).radical := by
        rw [qZ_radical_zero]; exact Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self 2
      have h3 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ (qZ 1).radical := hle h2
      rw [qZ_radical_one, Ideal.mem_span_singleton] at h3
      omega
    · rfl
  have hf0 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ (qZ 0).radical ↔ (0 : Fin 2) ∉ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)) := by
    rw [qZ_radical_zero, Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
    simp
  have hf1 : (2 : ℤ) ∈ (qZ 1).radical ↔ (1 : Fin 2) ∉ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)) := by
    rw [qZ_radical_one, Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
    simp
  have hf : ∀ i, (2 : ℤ) ∈ (qZ i).radical ↔ i ∉ ({1} : Finset (Fin 2)) := by
    intro i; fin_cases i
    · exact hf0
    · exact hf1
  have h := solution (Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)}) qZ {1} 2 hprimary hmin hirr hdecomp hisolated hf
  rw [inf_singleton_one] at h
  refine ⟨h.1, ?_⟩
  ext x
  rw [Submodule.mem_colon_singleton, smul_eq_mul, Ideal.mem_span_singleton,
    Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
  omega

/-- The bound in Exercise 4.15 is sharp in this example: `(12 : 2) = (6) ≠ (3) = q_Σ`. -/
theorem colon_one_int : (Ideal.span {(12 : ℤ)}).colon {2 ^ 1} = Ideal.span {(6 : ℤ)} := by
  ext x
  rw [Submodule.mem_colon_singleton, smul_eq_mul, Ideal.mem_span_singleton,
    Ideal.mem_span_singleton]
  constructor <;> intro h <;> omega

end Ex_4_15

end AtiyahMcdonald69

